+## The Option Help Bracket
+
+The bracket at the end of an option's help line, such as `[env var: NAME; default: 1; required]`, is built from items.
+{attr}`Option.help_extra_keys` lists their keys in the order they are shown. Each key has a method
+`get_help_extra_<key>(ctx)` that returns its value, or `None` to leave the item out, and
+{meth}`Option.format_help_extra_item` turns a value into the text in the bracket.
+
+A subclass adds an item by adding its key and writing its method. An item of a new key is shown as `<key>: <value>`:
+
+```python
+class UnitOption(click.Option):
+    help_extra_keys = (*click.Option.help_extra_keys, "unit")
+
+    def __init__(self, *args, unit=None, **kwargs):
+        super().__init__(*args, **kwargs)
+        self.unit = unit
+
+    def get_help_extra_unit(self, ctx):
+        return self.unit
+
+@click.command()
+@click.option("--timeout", cls=UnitOption, unit="s", default=30, show_default=True)
+def cli(timeout):
+    ...
+```
+
+The help line of `--timeout` ends with `[default: 30; unit: s]`. To change how an item is shown, override
+`get_help_extra_<key>` (its value) or `format_help_extra_item` (its text). {func}`click.formatting.append_help_extra`
+appends the bracket to a help text in the same form, for parameter classes of your own.
+
 ## Click's Wrapping Behavior
 
 Click's default wrapping ignores single new lines and rewraps the text based on the width of the terminal to a maximum of 80 characters by default, but this can be modified with {attr}`~Context.max_content_width`. In the example notice how the second grouping of three lines is rewrapped into a single paragraph.
 
